          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of the
            %em center of a monoidal category
            or the
            %em Drinfeld center
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            of the notion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/center"} center
            of a
            = succeed "(" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
          %p
            Where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/center"} center
            of a monoid is just a sub-monoid with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/property"} property
            that it commutes with everything else, under categorification this becomes a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} structure
            %em how
            the objects in the Drinfeld center commute (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"}> braid
            ) with everything else.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p We first give the general-abstract definition
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#AbstractDefinition"} Abstract definition
          %p of Drinfeld centers. Then we spell out what this means in components in
          %ul
